The agent door never declared its control cohort, so ":rush" and ":hood" seats
played an ordinary world while reporting that they were not.

m_randomCids is populated only by setRandomCountries, whose sole caller was
runAIEvaluation -- and AISystem gates BOTH the exploit variant and the scripted
rung on isRandomCountry(), which reads it:

    exploitHere = s_exploitVariant >= 0 && isRandomCountry(cid) && ...

Through the door the set was empty, every country failed that test, and nobody
ever rushed. The "[BENCH] ... ATTACKING without pause" line is printed from the
requested world string before any of this, so the log confirmed the request and
not the outcome. Measured before the fix: 1914:FRA:rung and 1914:FRA:rush
scored identically on 8 of 8 seeds under two different players, while
--eval-ai separates them 13.1 to 8.2. After it, at 120 turns and always-hold:
rung 8.6, rush 2.4, hood 0.4, with 1 of 3 bordering neighbours rushing.

The cohort logic is EXTRACTED rather than copied. A second copy of this rule
would drift silently, and the symptom of drift is a seat that scores plausibly
against the wrong opponent -- which is how this went unnoticed in the first
place.

